          <avActivityHighlights>Wind slabs require caution. Slab avalanches are possible.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>The wind slabs can still be released in some cases, in particular on very steep slopes. Backcountry tourers can release avalanches in some places, including medium-sized ones. &lt;br/&gt; On extreme sunny slopes and in steep rocky terrain small and, in isolated cases, medium-sized natural loose snow slides are to be expected as a consequence of warming during the day and solar radiation. &lt;br/&gt; Gliding avalanches can also occur, in particular on steep grassy slopes. Areas with glide cracks are to be avoided.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>In particular in the northwest 15 to 30 cm of snow fell in the last few days above approximately 1300 m. &lt;br/&gt; As a consequence of the occasionally strong northerly wind, the snow drift accumulations have increased in size during the last few days. &lt;br/&gt; Above approximately 2200 m there are 70 to 140 cm of snow, and even as much as 180 cm in some localities, especially above approximately 2500 m. &lt;br/&gt; During the sunny day, the surface snow becomes moist below 2500 m. In some places, where the wind has had a greater effect, it has a chalky consistency. &lt;br/&gt; It is not distinctly layered. Towards its base, the snowpack is moist, in particular below approximately 2300 m.</snowpackStructureComment>
          <tendencyComment>The weather conditions will facilitate a gradual strengthening of the snowpack.</tendencyComment>
