          <avActivityHighlights>Wind slabs require caution.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>The wind slabs of the last few days can still be released in some cases, in particular on very steep slopes. Adjacent to ridgelines and in gullies and bowls and at the base of rock walls and behind abrupt changes in the terrain the likelihood of avalanches is higher. &lt;br/&gt; As the day progresses the likelihood of dry and moist snow slides being released will increase on extremely steep sunny slopes. Mostly these are small.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>Over a wide area 15 to 20 cm of snow fell in the last few days above approximately 1300 m. &lt;br/&gt; Above approximately 2000 m there are 30 to 60 cm of snow, and even more in some localities. &lt;br/&gt; During the sunny day, the surface snow becomes moist below 2500 m. In some places, where the wind has had a greater effect, it has a chalky consistency. &lt;br/&gt; Towards its base, the snowpack is moist, in particular below approximately 2300 m. &lt;br/&gt; At low and intermediate altitudes only a little snow is lying.</snowpackStructureComment>
          <tendencyComment>The weather conditions will foster a gradual strengthening of the near-surface layers.</tendencyComment>
